2. The two block layouts are identical.\n- **Pair 3:** a 13×15 slab of 4 with 1-markers (a 2×2 block and a single cell). A 2-slab has the same 1-markers in the same relative layout. A 3×3 ring of 8 has a single 1 in its centre.\n\n**How the output is built.** The output is the largest piece, cropped to its bounding box. Every smaller piece is then pasted onto it, from largest to smallest. Each piece goes exactly where its marker cells line up with the same markers already on the canvas.\n\n- **Pair 1:** the 3/2/3 tile lands on rows 1–3. Its two 3-rows cover the slab's two 3-rows, and its 2-row replaces the 1-row between them.\n- **Pair 2:** the 1-slab lands at offset (2,2), where its four 2-blocks cover the 3-slab's four 2-blocks.\n- **Pair 3:** the 2-slab lands at (1,1), where its 1-markers sit on the 4-slab's 1-markers. Then the 8-ring lands with its central 1 on the single 1 at (6,5).\n\n**Making \"line up\" precise.** I score every offset that keeps the piece inside the canvas. I only look at cells where the canvas colour is one of the piece's own colours:\n\n- a cell where piece and canvas agree adds +1;\n- a cell where they disagree adds −1.\n\nCells where the canvas colour is foreign to the piece (for example, the host's fill colour) are ignored, so they can be covered freely. The best-scoring offset wins.\n\nThe −1 rule matters in pair 3. The 8-ring's centre 1 could also sit on a cell of the 2×2 block of 1s, which would score +1. But then the ring's 8s would cover the other three 1s of that block, giving three mismatches. The single 1 at (6,5) scores +1 with no mismatches, so it wins.\n\n**Applying it to the test.**\n\n1. The canvas is the 10×10 8-slab. Inside it are a 2-ring with an 8 centre and a 2-pair at relative row 7, columns 4–5.\n2. The 4×4 piece (a 2-ring with a 3 centre, with 3s along its right and bottom) uses colours {2,3}. It best matches over the slab's 2-ring, so it is pasted there. The 8 in the ring's centre is foreign to this piece, so the 3 can replace it without penalty.\n3. The 3×4 piece (a 4 frame around a 2-pair) uses colours {4,2}. It matches on the lone 2-pair. Any placement over the ring's 2s would put 4s on other 2s, which counts as mismatches, so it scores lower.\n\n**Teaching stages.** The program emits the cropped host first, then the canvas after each paste. The last emit is the final grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg, multicolour=True)\n\n    def area(o):\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n        return (y1 - y0 + 1) * (x1 - x0 + 1)\n\n    objs = sorted(objs, key=lambda o: -area(o))\n    y0, x0, y1, x1 = objs[0][\"box\"]\n    canvas = crop(g, y0, x0, y1, x1)\n    emit(canvas, \"Crop the largest piece; it becomes the output canvas.\")\n\n    H, W = canvas.shape\n    for o in objs[1:]:\n        oy0, ox0, oy1, ox1 = o[\"box\"]\n        piece = crop(g, oy0, ox0, oy1, ox1)\n        mask = np.zeros(piece.shape, bool)\n        for (y, x) in o[\"cells\"]:\n            mask[y - oy0, x - ox0] = True\n        cols = set(int(v) for v in piece[mask])\n        h, w = piece.shape\n        best = None\n        for dy in range(H - h + 1):\n            for dx in range(W - w + 1):\n                win = canvas[dy:dy + h, dx:dx + w]\n                rel = mask & np.isin(win, list(cols))\n                match = int(np.sum(rel & (win == piece)))\n                mis = int(np.sum(rel & (win != piece)))\n                s = match - mis\n                if best is None or s > best[0]:\n                    best = (s, dy, dx)\n        if best is None:\n            continue\n        _, dy, dx = best\n        region = canvas[dy:dy + h, dx:dx + w]\n        region[mask] = piece[mask]\n        emit(canvas, f\"Paste the {h}x{w} piece at ({dy},{dx}) where its marker cells align with the canvas.\")\n\n    emit(canvas, \"Final assembled grid.\")\n    return canvas\n","candidate_tactics":{"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**How the output is built.** The output is the largest piece, cropped to its bounding box."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Every smaller piece is then pasted onto it, from largest to smallest."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Crop the largest piece; it becomes the output canvas."}],"movement":[{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Paste the 3x3 piece at (1,1) where its marker cells align with the canvas."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Paste the 8x9 piece at (2,2) where its marker cells align with the canvas."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Paste the 8x7 piece at (1,1) where its marker cells align with the canvas."}],"repeat":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Pair 1:** a 9×5 slab of 1s with two rows of 3s, and a 3×3 tile reading 3/2/3."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Pair 1:** the 3/2/3 tile lands on rows 1–3."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Any placement over the ring's 2s would put 4s on other 2s, which counts as mismatches, so it scores lower."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**How the output is built.** The output is the largest piece, cropped to its bounding box."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Teaching stages.** The program emits the cropped host first, then the canvas after each paste."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Crop the largest piece; it becomes the output canvas."}],"assembly":[{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Final assembled grid."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"585aedc3b1806ce910cf9a147fd7aeae0d2b95a96d605d04b2e30a326dc2f69f","task_sha256":"3b95e7a4281f6c325d1846caa6b0ff0f41d512c9545cbae8aad84644bde8e33e","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
